-0.0218963623046875, -0.0019588470458984375, 0.0158233642578125, -0.0054168701171875, -0.0242462158203125, 0.004528045654296875, -0.00986480712890625, -0.01385498046875, 0.038238525390625, 0.0025882720947265625, 0.0280914306640625, 0.00020933151245117188, 0.0193939208984375, -0.0252227783203125, 0.02398681640625, 0.029052734375, 0.00665283203125, 0.0218963623046875, -0.0325927734375, -0.006641387939453125, 0.0191192626953125, -0.0105133056640625, -0.015869140625, 0.0104522705078125, 0.0156707763671875, 0.0131683349609375, -0.024200439453125, -0.00031638145446777344, -0.0249176025390625, 0.0179901123046875, 0.030548095703125, -0.0206298828125, 0.01081085205078125, 0.0091552734375, -0.023193359375, 0.0211029052734375, -0.0266265869140625, -0.017303466796875, 0.0250244140625, 0.0008826255798339844, 0.0247344970703125, 0.0060882568359375, 0.0213623046875, 0.035888671875, -0.00848388671875, -0.01448822021484375, -0.005527496337890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose  \nTo develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance (QA).\n\nMaterials and Methods  \n\nOverview of Workflow  \nThis retrospective study assessed a two-stage AI pipeline for structuring and QA of radiology reports. The system first segmented reports into anatomical sections using a rule-based agent, then refined assignments and performed QA tasks using a local large language model (LLM) agent. Performance was evaluated by radiologist review of a stratified sample of reports.\n\nStudy Design and Data  \nA total of 638 radiology reports from CT chest, abdomen, and pelvis exams, dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists at a single institution in 2023\u20132024, were included. Reports were randomly sampled for evaluation.\n\nMulti-Agent AI Pipeline Architecture  \n\nRule-Based Agent  \nThe first agent used regular expressions (regex) to identify and segment sentences into standardized anatomical sections (e.g., lungs, liver, kidneys). Regex patterns such as `r\"\\b(lungs?|pulmonary)\\b\"` and `r\"\\b(liver|hepatic)\\b\"` were iteratively developed by reviewing 100 randomly selected local reports. The full list of regex patterns, along with their development and refinement process, is provided in Supplementary Appendix A. Patterns were refined to maximize recall (target >95% of anatomical section headers correctly identified) and minimize false positives (target <5% incorrect assignments), as measured by manual review on a validation set of 50 reports. Performance was tracked by calculating the proportion of correctly and incorrectly assigned sentences per section.\n\nLLM Agent and Prompt Engineering  \nA 7B-parameter Llama-2 model, running on-premises, was used to resolve ambiguous assignments and perform semantic QA checks. The LLM was prompted with institution-specific instructions. For example, initial prompts were: \u201cAssign the following sentence to the most appropriate anatomical section: [sentence].\u201d Iterative prompt engineering included adding explicit lists of local section headers and reporting conventions, e.g., \u201cUse the following section list: [list]. If uncertain, assign to \u2018Other\u2019.\u201d The full set of prompt versions and engineering steps, including criteria for refinement and radiologist feedback, is detailed in Supplementary Appendix B. Radiologist feedback was incorporated by having two radiologists review LLM outputs on 30 validation reports, rating accuracy and providing suggestions. Prompts were revised until >90% agreement with expert assignments was achieved on the validation set.\n\nAgent Interaction and QA Tasks  \nA controller script orchestrated the pipeline: the regex agent generated preliminary section assignments, which the LLM agent reviewed and refined. The LLM also performed three QA tasks using prompt-based logic:  \n(a) Section mismatch: \u201cAre there findings in the Findings section not reflected in the Impression, or vice versa?\u201d  \n(b) Gender-anatomy conflict: \u201cDoes the report mention anatomical findings inconsistent with the patient\u2019s gender?\u201d  \n(c) Undocumented critical findings: \u201cIs there mention of a critical finding, and if so, is communication documented?\u201d  \nPrompts for each task were validated by comparing LLM outputs to expert annotations on 50 reports, with error rates (false positives/negatives) tracked and prompts revised to minimize errors (<10% for each task). The exact prompts and validation process are provided in Supplementary Appendix C.\n\nEvaluation Design and Metrics  \nA random subset of 45 reports was selected for radiologist evaluation, stratified to include 23 flagged and 22 unflagged reports, reflecting the overall flagged/unflagged ratio. The sample size was chosen to provide a 95% confidence interval of \u00b115% for the primary outcome (correct restructuring rate), based on pilot data showing 65\u201375% accuracy. A formal power analysis (see Supplementary Appendix D) indicated that, with 45 reports, the study had approximately 80% power to detect a 20% difference in correct restructuring rate from a null hypothesis of 50%. However, the wide confidence intervals and modest sample size limit the precision of secondary outcome estimates and the ability to detect rare errors. This limitation is discussed further below.\n\nEvaluation Criteria and Inter-Rater Reliability  \nCorrectness of restructuring was defined as all sentences assigned to correct anatomical sections, with no omission or fabrication of clinically important information. Clinically important information was operationalized as findings that, if omitted or misassigned, could alter patient management (e.g., new or significant lesions, critical findings, actionable findings as defined by ACR guidelines). The full rubric and examples are provided in Supplementary Appendix E. Two board-certified radiologists independently assessed:  \n(1) Correctness of restructuring, using the rubric above.  \n(2) QA task performance\u2014each flagged issue was rated as true/false positive/negative.  \n(3) Omission or fabrication of clinically important information\u2014examples included missing documentation of a pulmonary nodule or erroneous addition of a finding.  \nDisagreements were resolved by consensus discussion, with each case discussed until agreement was reached. The frequency and nature of disagreements were recorded and categorized as section assignment, omission, fabrication, or ambiguous language. A granular breakdown of disagreements and their resolution is provided in Supplementary Appendix F.\n\nSystem Requirements and Deployment  \nThe system was deployed on a workstation with dual NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPUs, 256 GB RAM, and Ubuntu 22.04. LLM inference averaged 2.5 seconds per report; regex processing was <0.1 seconds per report. To benchmark feasibility, we tested the system on a standard clinical workstation (Intel Xeon CPU, 64 GB RAM, NVIDIA RTX 3060 GPU), where LLM inference time increased to 12.8 seconds per report. This hardware is more representative of typical clinical infrastructure. Model quantization and use of smaller models (e.g., 3B-parameter LLMs) reduced inference time by 40\u201360% with minimal loss in accuracy (see Supplementary Appendix G). The system operated as a standalone batch processor and was not integrated into clinical workflow. Integration strategies and optimization steps for deployment are discussed in the Limitations section.\n\nResults  \n\nReport Structuring  \nThe system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format, retaining original content. Of the 45 evaluated reports, both reviewers agreed that 31 (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2 (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and 12 (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%) had reviewer disagreement. Correct restructuring required all sentences to be assigned to the correct anatomical section, with no omission or fabrication of clinically important information. Cohen\u2019s kappa for restructuring correctness was 0.62, indicating substantial agreement. Disagreements most commonly involved borderline cases of section assignment (8/12), omission of minor findings (3/12), or ambiguous language (1/12). A detailed breakdown of disagreement types and representative examples is provided in Supplementary Appendix F.\n\nQuality Assurance Task Performance  \nConfusion matrices for each QA task, with 95% confidence intervals for sensitivity and specificity (calculated using the Wilson method), are shown below:\n\n| QA Task                  | True Pos | False Pos | True Neg | False Neg | Sensitivity (95% CI) | Specificity (95% CI) |\n|--------------------------|----------|-----------|----------|-----------|----------------------|----------------------|\n| Section Mismatch         |   10     |     2     |   31     |     2     | 0.83 (0.52\u20130.98)     | 0.94 (0.80\u20130.99)     |\n| Gender-Anatomy Conflict  |    2     |     0     |   43     |     0     | 1.00 (0.16\u20131.00)     | 1.00 (0.92\u20131.00)     |\n| Undocumented Critical    |    1     |     0     |   44     |     0     | 1.00 (0.03\u20131.00)     | 1.00 (0.92\u20131.00)     |\n\nFor section mismatches, false positives (2/14 flagged) were due to ambiguous language or non-standard section headers (e.g., \u201cupper abdomen\u201d instead of \u201cliver\u201d or \u201cspleen\u201d). False negatives (2/12 actual mismatches) occurred when findings were described in non-standard phrasing (e.g., \u201csmall hypodensity in right lobe\u201d not mapped to \u201cliver\u201d). For gender-anatomy conflicts, both flagged cases were true positives (e.g., mention of \u201cprostate\u201d in a female patient due to template error). For undocumented critical findings, the single flagged case was a true positive (e.g., \u201clarge pulmonary embolism\u201d not documented as communicated).\n\nError Analysis  \nOf the 90 reports flagged in the full dataset, 80 (12.5%) were for section mismatches, 7 (1.1%) for gender-anatomy conflicts, and 3 (0.5%) for undocumented critical findings. Section mismatches were categorized as:  \n- Omission of benign findings from Impression (54/80, 67.5%; e.g., \u201cmild atelectasis\u201d not summarized)  \n- Omission of potentially clinically significant findings (14/80, 17.5%; e.g., \u201cpulmonary nodule\u201d not in Impression)  \n- Misassignment to incorrect anatomical section (12/80, 15%; e.g., \u201cadrenal nodule\u201d placed under \u201ckidneys\u201d)  \nRoot causes included language ambiguity (38%; e.g., \u201cright upper quadrant mass\u201d not mapped), atypical or missing section headers (27%), template errors (15%), and rare anatomical variants or complex sentence structure (20%). For each error category, mitigation strategies are proposed in Supplementary Appendix H, including expanding regex coverage,"}}